[[typeclasses]]
=== Overload terms with typeclasses

A typeclass lets one name have a different implementation for each type it is
applied to. Where term polymorphism gives the compiler a free choice between
interchangeable definitions, a typeclass instance is _selected_ by the type at
the call site. The idea is the same as typeclasses in Haskell, traits in Rust,
interfaces in Java, and concepts in {cpp}.

A class declares method signatures. An instance supplies the implementations
for one type:

[source, morloc]
.pretty.loc
----
module main (describeInts, describeReals)

import root-py

class Pretty a where
    pretty :: a -> Str

instance Pretty Int
       , Pretty Real where
    source Py from "ops.py" ("to_str" as pretty)

title :: Pretty a => a -> Str
title x = "value: " <> pretty x

describeInts :: [Int] -> [Str]
describeInts = map title

describeReals :: [Real] -> [Str]
describeReals = map title
----

[source, python]
.ops.py
----
def to_str(x):
    return str(x)
----

[source, console]
----
$ morloc make -o pretty pretty.loc
$ ./pretty describeInts '[1,2]'
["value: 1","value: 2"]
$ ./pretty describeReals '[1.5]'
["value: 1.5"]
----


==== One class, many languages

An instance may source implementations from several languages at once. The
compiler then has a choice of instance bodies for the same method, and the
usual collapse applies: it takes whichever one keeps the program in one
language.

[source, morloc]
----
class Addable a where
    zero :: a
    (+) :: a -> a -> a

instance Addable Int where
    source Py from "arithmetic.py" ("add" as (+))
    source Cpp from "arithmetic.hpp" ("add" as (+))
    zero = 0

instance Addable Real where
    source Py from "arithmetic.py" ("add" as (+))
    source Cpp from "arithmetic.hpp" ("add" as (+))
    zero = 0.0
----

The native functions may be polymorphic in their own language, in which case
the same implementation is named by several instances. The Python `add` above
is one function:

[source, python]
.arithmetic.py
----
def add(x, y):
    return x + y
----

And so is the {cpp} one:

[source, cpp]
.arithmetic.hpp
----
template <class A>
A add(A x, A y){
    return x + y;
}
----

A method does not have to come from a foreign language. `zero = 0` is an
ordinary Morloc definition, and it is polymorphic in the same way any other
term is: `zero` in the `Int` instance is the integer literal, `zero` in the
`Real` instance is the floating-point one.

[WARNING]
.This example collides with `root`
====

`Addable` redeclares `zero` and `(+)`, which `root` already supplies through
its `Integral` class. Two classes cannot define the same term, so this module
compiles only in isolation -- with `import internal` for the primitive types,
not `import root-py`:

[source, console]
----
$ morloc typecheck main.loc
In module 'main': The typeclasses 'Integral' and 'Addable' have conflicting definitions of the term 'zero'
----

====

==== Superclasses

A class may require another class. Write the requirement to the left of `+=>+`
in the class head:

[source, morloc]
----
class Pretty a => Boxed a where
    box :: a -> Str
----

Any type with a `Boxed` instance must also have a `Pretty` instance, and a
function constrained on `Boxed a` may use `pretty` as well as `box`. This is
how `root` layers its numeric hierarchy: `+class Integral a => Numeric a+`
means every `Numeric` type is also `Integral`.

==== Instances with contexts

An instance for a type with parameters may need instances at those
parameters. Declare them before `+=>+`:

[source, morloc]
----
instance Sz a => Sz (List a) where
    sz xs = fold (\n x -> n + sz x) 0 xs
----

Inside the body, `sz x` at `a` is allowed because of `Sz a`, and
`Sz (List Int)` exists wherever `Sz Int` does.

==== Importing a class from another module

A class is exported and imported by its name. Its methods are not separately
importable and may not appear in an export list:

[source, morloc]
.numops/main.loc
----
module numops (Pretty, exclaim)

import root

class Pretty a where
    pretty :: a -> Str

exclaim :: Pretty a => a -> Str
exclaim x = pretty x <> "!"
----

Listing `pretty` in that export list gives
`Module '.numops' does not export the following terms or types: [pretty]`,
which is confusing but means what it says: a method has no standalone
identity to export.

Importing the class name is enough to declare instances for it elsewhere:

[source, morloc]
.main.loc
----
module main (shout)

import root-py
import .numops (Pretty, exclaim)

instance Pretty Int where
    source Py from "ops.py" ("to_str" as pretty)

shout :: Int -> Str
shout = exclaim
----

[source, console]
----
$ morloc make -o prog main.loc
$ ./prog shout 7
"7!"
----

This is the shape every language-specific standard library module takes. `root`
declares `Eq`, `Ord`, `Functor`, `Foldable` and the rest; `root-py`, `root-cpp`
and `root-r` import those names and fill in instances. Nothing in `root` knows
which languages exist.

[[deriving]]
==== Deriving instances

Some instances follow from the shape of a type alone. Equality, ordering, a
printed form and a hash can be defined once for every value Morloc can
serialize, so you do not have to write them. A `deriving` clause on a `data`,
`record` or `newtype` declaration asks the compiler for them:

[source, morloc]
.shapes.loc
----
module main (sameShape, larger, describe, fingerprint)

import root-py

data Shape = Circle Real | Rect Real Real
  deriving (Eq, Ord, Show, Hashable)

sameShape :: Shape -> Shape -> Bool
sameShape a b = a == b

larger :: Shape -> Shape -> Shape
larger = max

describe :: Shape -> Str
describe = show

fingerprint :: Shape -> Str
fingerprint = hash
----

[source, console]
----
$ morloc make -o shapes shapes.loc
$ ./shapes sameShape '{"Circle":[1.0]}' '{"Circle":[1]}'
true
$ ./shapes larger '{"Rect":[2,1]}' '{"Circle":[3]}'
{"Rect":[2,1]}
$ ./shapes describe '{"Rect":[2,1]}'
"{\"Rect\":[2,1]}"
$ ./shapes fingerprint '{"Rect":[2,1]}'
"e1a2ddc07d5d9530"
----

No foreign code was written, and none is needed. A derived instance works on
the value in the form it takes when it crosses between languages, the same
form the command line reads and prints. The instances of the fields are never
consulted, so the answers do not depend on which language runs the
comparison. Swap `root-py` for `root-cpp` or `root-r` and every line above
prints the same thing, hash included.

The classes you can derive are these:

`Eq`:: `==` is structural equality.
`Ord`:: `+<=+` compares fields left to right. A `data` value orders by the
  position of its constructor in the declaration first, so every `Circle` is
  less than every `Rect`, and then by its fields. Lists compare element by
  element, strings by their UTF-8 bytes, and an absent optional value sorts
  before a present one.
`Show`:: `show` gives the JSON text the command line would print.
`Hashable`:: `hash` gives a hex string, equal for equal values.
`Packable`:: Only on a `newtype`; see below.

Floats are compared by their bits, not by IEEE equality: `NaN == NaN` is true
and `-0.0 == 0.0` is false, and `Ord` puts `-0.0` before `0.0`. That keeps
the rule that equal values have equal hashes.

[WARNING]
.Derived equality compares the serialized form as it is
====

A type whose serialized form is not unique gets an equality that can tell
equal things apart. A `Map` crosses languages as a list of pairs, so two maps
with the same entries inserted in different orders are not `==` under a
derived instance. If your type needs equality up to some normal form, write
the instance yourself.

====

A deriving clause and a hand-written instance of the same class for the same
type is an error, since the compiler cannot know which one you meant:

[source, console]
----
$ morloc make -o both both.loc
both.loc:7:1: error:
The instance Eq (Name) is also derived by its type's deriving clause; keep one.
  |
7 | instance Eq Name where
  | ^
----

And any other class is rejected where it is named:

[source, console]
----
$ morloc make -o other other.loc
other.loc:5:30: cannot derive 'Functor'; a declaration may derive Eq, Ord, Show and Hashable, and a newtype also Packable
    |
  5 | newtype Name = Str deriving (Functor)
    |                              ^
----

===== Deriving `Packable` for a `newtype`

A `newtype` (<<newtype>>) is a new type that crosses languages in the same
form as the type on its right-hand side. Converting between the two goes
through `Packable`: `pack` builds the newtype from its representation, and
`unpack` takes it apart.

`unpack` you always get. Every `newtype` with a right-hand side can be
unwrapped, with no declaration at all. `pack` you get by deriving
`Packable`:

[source, morloc]
.temp.loc
----
module main (fromReal, toReal)

import root-py

newtype Celsius = Real deriving (Packable)

fromReal :: Real -> Celsius
fromReal = pack

toReal :: Celsius -> Real
toReal = unpack
----

[source, console]
----
$ morloc make -o temp temp.loc
$ ./temp fromReal 21.5
21.5
$ ./temp toReal 21.5
21.5
----

Neither conversion changes the value. A `Celsius` is a `Real` on the wire, so
the conversion is free when it happens as part of passing the value to another
language, and it is free inside a language too as long as `Celsius` declares
no native form of its own there. If it does, say `+type Py => Celsius =
"MyCelsius"+`, then something has to build that object. The compiler moves
the conversion to a boundary with a language where `Celsius` has no form of
its own, and when the program has no such language it stops:

[source, console]
----
$ morloc make -o formed formed.loc
formed.loc:5:1: error:
Cannot convert between 'Celsius' and its representation in py: Celsius has a py form of its own, and no language without one is available.
  |
5 | newtype Celsius = Real deriving (Packable)
  | ^
----

The fix is a hand-written `Packable` instance that sources the conversion in
that language, in place of the `deriving` clause.

Leave out `deriving (Packable)` and `pack` is not defined:

[source, console]
----
$ morloc make -o nopack nopack.loc
nopack.loc:8:12: error:
General type error: No instance found for Packable::pack at type Real -> Celsius
  The newtype 'Celsius' has no 'pack'; add 'deriving (Packable)' to its declaration or declare a Packable instance.
  |
8 | fromReal = pack
  |            ^
----

That is deliberate: the point of a `newtype` is that you cannot create one by
accident.

`pack` is derivable only when the right-hand side fixes every parameter of
the newtype. A dimension that appears only on the left cannot be recovered
from the list it wraps, so this is rejected:

[source, console]
----
$ morloc make -o sized sized.loc
sized.loc:5:47: 'Sized' cannot derive Packable: its representation does not determine 'n'
    |
  5 | newtype Sized (n :: Nat) a = List a deriving (Packable)
    |                                               ^
----

For a type like that, write the `Packable` instance by hand, as the standard
library does for `Vector`.

